# Enable connections

Read the hosted Tilde `enable-connections` skill before acting. It is the canonical process guide: https://docs.trytilde.ai/llms/connections.md.

1. Identify the authenticated target user and your agent bundle. Search enabled capabilities for both scopes and verify the correct account. Reuse an already usable account without prompting for setup again.
2. If a new account is needed, decide whether it belongs to the user or this bot. If unclear, ask whether other bots should have access: yes generally means personal/user, no generally means bot-owned. Preserve that choice in native resource creation and MCP mappings.
3. Discover provider and credential-source IDs, then invoke native Tilde creation/brokering. Do not call a preliminary account-picker tool or create a capability proposal. The pending resource and `connector_setup_required` result drive the enable-provider card.
4. In API chat clients, the card opens secure setup modals for that existing resource. In WhatsApp, iMessage, Slack and other external channels, invoke sendMessage with the server-returned broker or hosted setup URL. Never invent URLs or request secrets in chat.
5. Wait for native setup completion. Enable only required tools and map them to the selected MCP. Use personal resource APIs for personal accounts; use your own bundle for bot-owned resources. Native permissions apply to every call.
6. Verify account status and mappings, then resume the task. Reconcile existing IDs before retrying partially completed operations.

Use speaker-scoped federation to access user tools. Do not copy user credentials into an agent bundle, silently change ownership, or widen permissions to bypass an authorization failure.
